Barosaurus was a long-necked plant-eating dinosaur, a close relative of Diplodocus, that lived in the American West about 150 million years ago. The skeleton behind the American Museum of Natural History's famous mount was dug up by Earl Douglass in Utah in the 1920s and was at first mistaken for a Diplodocus. Its bones ended up split between three separate museums, until Barnum Brown bought all three portions for the AMNH around 1930. The skeleton then sat mostly ignored for sixty years.

During a major renovation in the late 1980s, two museum staff independently hit on the idea of showing a sauropod rearing up on its hind legs — an idea partly inspired by a Gregory Paul painting. A Canadian company made lightweight casts and assembled them into what is still the tallest free-standing mounted skeleton in the world, about 15 metres tall. Roughly 80% of the cast comes from the real specimen; most of the rest, including part of the neck and the skull, comes from Diplodocus casts.

The pose was never meant as a firm claim about behaviour, but as a prompt for visitors to ask what fossils really can and cannot tell us. It sparked scientific argument — some of it in a medical journal, about how such an animal could pump blood to its raised head — and later biomechanical modelling suggested the pose was indeed feasible. The mount has since shaped how sauropods appear in films, television and toys.
